<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The nook itself: an alcove cut into the room, which also reads as a
  lowercase n.

  Same shape as logo.svg, moved and scaled to suit an adaptive icon rather
  than a web page. The web mark sits low in its box because the rounded square
  around it gives it a floor; here the launcher supplies the shape, so the
  alcove has to be centred on its own or it looks like it has slipped.

  The geometry, so the next person does not have to reverse it:
    logo.svg draws the alcove on a 96x96 canvas at x 32..64, y 46..74.
    Shifted to the origin and scaled by 1.1875, then placed at (35, 37.375),
    it spans x 35..73 and y 37.375..70.625 — 38 wide, 33 tall, centred on
    (54, 54), and 25.2 from the centre at its furthest corner.

  That last number is the one that matters. Adaptive icons guarantee only the
  middle 72x72 square, and launchers that mask to a circle show a 66 diameter
  — radius 33. At 25.2 the mark clears a circular mask comfortably, so it is
  never clipped no matter which launcher is installed.

  It is drawn smaller than it needs to be, and that is the point. Filling the
  safe zone was tried first: the alcove ran edge to edge and stopped reading
  as a recess cut into a wall — it became a plain terracotta arch with some
  bisque behind it. The margin is what makes the shape mean something.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    xmlns:aapt="http://schemas.android.com/aapt"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!--
      The alcove, with logo.svg's own gradient rather than a flat fill.

      It was flat #C0603C here first, which is the gradient's end colour, so the
      icon read a shade heavier and flatter than the mark everywhere else. The
      gradient runs the same direction as the web version (x 0→0.2, y 0→1 of
      the shape's box), which on this 38-wide, 33-tall alcove is 35,37 → 42,70.

      logo.svg also drops a soft shadow under the alcove. That is not
      reproduced: VectorDrawable has no blur, and the usual fake — a darker
      copy offset behind — turns to mud at 48px, which is the size that
      actually matters. Launchers apply their own elevation to the foreground
      layer anyway, so the depth arrives from the system rather than from a
      smudge baked into the asset.
    -->
    <path android:pathData="M35,70.625 V37.375 a19,19 0 0 1 38,0 V70.625 Z">
        <aapt:attr name="android:fillColor">
            <gradient
                android:type="linear"
                android:startX="35"
                android:startY="37.375"
                android:endX="42.6"
                android:endY="70.625">
                <item android:offset="0" android:color="#FFA9502F" />
                <item android:offset="1" android:color="#FFC0603C" />
            </gradient>
        </aapt:attr>
    </path>

    <!-- light catching the left wall, which is what stops it reading flat -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M35,70.625 V37.375 a19,19 0 0 1 7.8375,-15.3188 l4.0375,5.7 A13.0625,13.0625 0 0 0 42.125,37.375 V70.625 Z"
        android:fillColor="#FFFFFF"
        android:fillAlpha="0.18" />

    <!-- the floor, in shadow -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M35,65.875 h38 v4.75 h-38 Z"
        android:fillColor="#1E1A17"
        android:fillAlpha="0.18" />
</vector>
